Behaviour matched in the engine, from reading another program's source and writing our
own (no code copied; a line each so a reader knows what was looked at):

  crates/gardenfell-rules     the .ini configuration format of Yacoby's Groundcover
  crates/gardenfell-scatter   Generator (https://github.com/Yacoby/mw-groundcover-generator),
                              which Gardenfell imports, and the placement conventions a
                              config written for it expects (the height offset, the
                              texture matching, the scale and rotation randomisation),
                              kept so that the community's configs mean here what they
                              meant there; where Gardenfell differs on purpose the code
                              says so at the spot
  crates/gardenfell-tes3      the record and subrecord layouts of the TES3 file formats,
  crates/gardenfell-nif       and the NIF block types and flag values, were read from
                              Greatness7's tes3 (https://github.com/Greatness7/tes3,
                              its libs/nif/src/types/ for the NIF), with his permission
                              given to Robin; the readers here are our own
  crates/gardenfell-nif       how a NIF is read and drawn follows OpenMW's nifosg where
                              the format alone does not decide (controllers, the morpher,
                              the texture source byte, bounds)
  crates/gardenfell-world     the load-order merge follows what the game and OpenMW do
                              with deleted, moved and overridden references, read
                              against Greatness7's merge_to_master as well
                              (https://github.com/Greatness7/merge_to_master), with the
                              differences recorded in esp-format.md
  crates/gardenfell-install   plugins.rs orders the addons an openmw.cfg does not select
                              as OpenMW's launcher orders them, written from that
                              description (alphabetical, each master pulled up to just
                              before the first file naming it, Bloodmoon after Tribunal);
                              renderer.rs reads an OpenMW install's settings.cfg with the
                              defaults OpenMW ships in settings-default.cfg (values, which
                              are data), and computes a light's attenuation as
                              NiPointLight::setAttenuationForRadius does, which MWSE
                              (MIT) decompiles and OpenMW's configureLight reproduces
  page-source/25_sky.js       the time-of-day interpolation of colours is OpenMW's
                              TimeOfDayInterpolator, which reproduces the game's; the
                              scattering constants are MGE XE's own (distantinit.cpp, data)
                              and the fog distances follow how MGE XE turns its ini values
                              into start and end (adjustFog), a formula and not its code,
                              and OpenMW's FogManager::configure for an OpenMW install.
                              The file is MIT.
  page-source/27_weather.js   the weather particles are placed and turned as OpenMW's
  page-source/28_particles.js SkyManager and nifosg do
  page-source/10_preview.js   the controller cycle functions (interpKey, the Cycle,
                              Reverse and Clamp modes, the slerp) follow OpenMW's

Data:

  The light attenuation defaults and the OpenMW settings defaults are the values those
  programs ship. The fixture install under fixture/ is generated by mkfixture.js and every
  plugin, mesh and texture under page-source/ has a generator beside it (t_fixtures
  checks); none of it is Bethesda's. Three meshes some tests read when they are present,
  ashcloud.nif and ex_stronghold_wall00.nif (Bethesda, out of Morrowind.bsa) and
  rr_basket.nif (RR Better Meshes), sit only on the machine the tests run on and are kept
  out of the repository by .gitignore; without them those tests say so and skip.
